Z||000000||contra||09-05-2000||06:05 PM||||I see tons of fieros now in toronto<BR>all of the red or maroon, i hardly see any ones of any other colours on the street<BR>the thing is, they're all 1984 2m4's.<BR>i think i've only seen 2 other GT's on the street other than my own. At fiero shop you see like over a dozen lined up, but i never see em except there. I'm wondering why the 2m4 is so popular, and nobody really has any newer ones, or any V6's. Ontop of all that, the 2m4's all seem to be in bad shape which is a shame too. I had one guy today in his red 2m4 staring my fiero left right and center in disbelief that they could look so nice. I hope it encourages at least one person to at 'least' paint their fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>but i'll settle for a wash and wax from them ..<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-05-2000||06:47 PM||||84's seem to be everywhere because more than 1 in 3 Fieros ever built was an 84!  (136,840 84's out of 370,168 total production.  Source: Corbin's Fiero Spotter's Guide)<P>But you're right, Contra - the rattiest Fieros you see seem to be 84's.  I don't know why people don't take as much pride in them - after all, the 84 started it all!  That's why it always makes me happy to see a well-preserved 84 like Ot Huster's, SloPonti's or Homicidal's.||||reg||9
